186308174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186307357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308174 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308174 #edb/ref 1077 "field-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308174 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186305675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308174 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d21c0b5c-0ae9-5804-9b21-0de9938b2f4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308231 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308231 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308231 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186307248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308231 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186307278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308231 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308231 #edb/ref 1077 "field-7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308231 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186307248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308231 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e18149d5-ec41-595e-a036-6b11138bd767" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308431 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308431 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308431 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308431 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186308431 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ecc3793-6a18-51b0-b6dd-b12969e4e994" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308431 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/6b/6bdaece2579041f23a73323c4317751dfd8266364633bd2af258437b53d4a6f9.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186308433 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308433 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333295" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308433 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308433 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186308433 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e759507e-a8b9-5e12-8e59-4730d3a76a6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308433 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince ${e}^{-5t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/7b/7be65c63a4e5dcbe73fbc63faf3f061476ecd478cef8d58776bb843bfe84df2c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186308471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308471 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333602" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308471 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308471 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-5x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-4x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/55/558ff2994d715139bde11747fdffddbbbd20f17f80c8deb3b90f4ae76078bdba.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={{field-6}} \\\\ y &=(2t+1){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-7}} | $0$ | $0.11$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-8}} | $1$ | $0.45$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/10/10050fff096c999bdd1d845a247dac87a3135458313338f62018612639a011b0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c2/c227e135e04b76f093d8c2ad6e2a4365e206c19b281f1a3df7a4b65dd479f411.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186308471 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42934189-fc99-5bad-a039-803cf217aa1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308471 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/55/558ff2994d715139bde11747fdffddbbbd20f17f80c8deb3b90f4ae76078bdba.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ y &=(2t+1){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.24$ | $0$ | $0.11$ |\n| $y$ | $0.00$ | $1$ | $0.45$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/10/10050fff096c999bdd1d845a247dac87a3135458313338f62018612639a011b0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c2/c227e135e04b76f093d8c2ad6e2a4365e206c19b281f1a3df7a4b65dd479f411.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186308477 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308477 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308477 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333620" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308477 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308477 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186308477 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f8861ee7-e573-5d09-b7e5-a9dfbae7172d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308477 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/97/97a85038b240896686ef1a796d81acc6aa52d01627f3aeca4268a309badc4867.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186391219 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391219 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391219 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333614" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391219 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391219 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186391219 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04b55cbe-b4be-543b-bbe4-7f5a1dc7e192" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391219 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c1/c1d7f49dfe79bcd36394e4b0bb49180167ac7a8791555f0f6974f5fe49fa249f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186391220 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391220 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391220 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391220 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391220 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186391222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391220 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "23101e77-153a-53ab-8eaa-fb91f151bfef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391221 #edb/ref 1080 "star" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391221 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391221 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1750bded-03a2-535d-9322-af370f69f5d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391222 #edb/ref 1080 "degenerate" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391222 #edb/